for three decades governments around the  world have met every year to forge a  global response to the climate emergency  and a treaty bound under the 1992 united  nations framework convention on climate  change to commit to that  this year it's the 26th iteration of the  un's cop which stands for the conference  of the parties and is seen by many as  our last chance to make a difference to  the future of our planet  the summit is being hosted by the uk and  italy in glasgow and thousands of people  197 parties that's 196 countries plus  the eu  planning to attend  now to get an idea of what agreements  it's hoped will be achieved we need to  look back to cop 21 where there was the  largest gathering of world leaders in  history  and for the first time ever nearly every  country on earth signed the paris  climate agreement now that agreed to  limit global warming to below two  degrees celsius with a hope for one and  a half degrees  to sign the paris agreement though  countries didn't need to tell us how  they would get there but this time they  do  and they have to submit their plans  known as nationally determined  contributions or ndcs  now cop president alok sharma has  challenged some of the world's biggest  emitters china india saudi arabia and  australia to deliver on promises they  made and come up with better climate  targets  and that's because the united nations  has warned the world is facing 2.7  degrees of warming due to some countries  plans simply not going far enough  so now the world turns to cop 26  where the un secretary general says the  era of pledges is over  now is the time to close the emissions  gap and stop a climate catastrophe
 

